/**
\defgroup karma_core Core
Foundational identifiers, type IDs, math helpers, and timing utilities.

Core code has no dependency on higher Karma layers. Types here are intentionally
small and suitable for inclusion by any other public header.
*/

/**
\defgroup karma_world World
ECS, data components, scene graph, and system graph contracts.

The world layer owns gameplay-facing data. Rendering, simulation, content, and
feature modules consume this data, but components themselves should not call
higher-level systems.
*/

/**
\defgroup karma_world_ecs ECS
\ingroup karma_world
Sparse-set entity/component storage and entity lifetime management.

Use karma::ecs::World for normal gameplay code. Lower-level storage types are
public so tests, tools, and specialized systems can inspect dense component
layouts when needed.
*/

/**
\defgroup karma_components Components
\ingroup karma_world
ECS components consumed by runtime systems.

Components are plain data contracts. Systems own behavior and derived runtime
state. When adding a component, document the system that writes it, the systems
that read it, and whether it is author-authored, runtime-authored, or both.
*/

/**
\defgroup karma_scene Scene Graph
\ingroup karma_world
Scene-node ownership and transform hierarchy helpers.

Karma keeps local transform data and final world transforms separate. Scene
hierarchy code composes local transforms into world-space TransformComponent
values before render, physics, lights, audio, and particle systems consume them.
*/

/**
\defgroup karma_systems Systems
\ingroup karma_world
Frame-updated ECS systems and dependency ordering.

Use SystemGraph for simple system registration and dependency sorting. Engine
owned systems are wired by runtime::EngineApp; optional visual features are
usually exposed as RuntimeModule implementations.
*/

/**
\defgroup karma_runtime Runtime
Application lifecycle, engine composition, input, UI context, debug overlay, and
scene helper utilities.

Runtime code is allowed to wire lower layers together. Game code generally
interacts with EngineApp, GameInterface, RuntimeModule, and UI layer contracts.
*/

/**
\defgroup karma_rendering Rendering
Renderer-facing APIs, material/mesh/texture handles, UI draw submission, scene
extraction, particle submission, and backend abstractions.

Gameplay code should prefer ECS components and high-level helpers. Use renderer
types directly for resources, diagnostics, picking, debug drawing, custom
runtime modules, or named post-process profile registration.

See \ref karma_rendering_guide for camera-resolved post-processing and render
submission details.
*/

/**
\defgroup karma_simulation Simulation
Animation, physics, collision, and navigation APIs.

Simulation systems write back to ECS components rather than owning gameplay
state invisibly. Navigation and animation are designed to remain renderer
agnostic.
*/

/**
\defgroup karma_animation Animation
\ingroup karma_simulation
Animation clips, sampling, pose math, morph targets, and deformation support.

Node animation writes local transforms. Scene hierarchy composition writes
world transforms. Imported skinned and morphed meshes use GPU deformation by
default, while CPU deformation remains a correctness/reference path.

See \ref karma_animation_guide for the glTF animation and deformation
runtime flow.
*/

/**
\defgroup karma_physics Physics
\ingroup karma_simulation
Physics backend interfaces, rigid bodies, character controllers, and physics
world/system integration.

Karma supports Jolt through public physics abstractions. The Bullet backend is
experimental and may lag the ECS physics feature set.
*/

/**
\defgroup karma_navigation Navigation
\ingroup karma_simulation
Solo/tiled navmesh baking, Detour queries, tile-cache obstacles, crowd steering,
geometry collection, diagnostics, and the engine-owned async navigation system.

Navmesh generation can collect geometry from glTF/GLB scenes, ECS mesh colliders, or
direct mesh data. Runtime path requests are queued through NavigationSystem;
tile-cache and crowd updates mutate live simulation state on the main thread.
*/

/**
\defgroup karma_content Content
Asset loading, image helpers, mesh import, glTF/GLB import, prefab instantiation, and
prefab-local resource registration.

Content APIs transform authored data into shared world contracts, ECS
components, and registered resource keys. They should not own per-frame
behavior.
*/

/**
\defgroup karma_prefabs Prefabs
\ingroup karma_content
JSON prefab loading, component serializer registration, prefab instantiation,
destruction, and sidecar resource management.

Prefab files describe entity composition and component payloads. Runtime
systems still own behavior; prefab sidecars register texture aliases, effects,
and other package-local resources needed for direct path-only instantiation.
*/

/**
\defgroup karma_features Features
Optional feature modules built on public lower-layer APIs.

Feature modules are the right home for reusable visual behavior that is not a
renderer backend primitive. Runtime participation should be exposed through
factories or RuntimeModule implementations.
*/

/**
\defgroup karma_particles Particles
\ingroup karma_features
Particle effect libraries, `.kpeffect` parsing, emitter binding helpers, and
runtime particle simulation.

Particle effects are authored as reusable emitter templates. Gameplay code
binds entities to effect keys and can apply per-instance overrides.
*/

/**
\defgroup karma_volumes Volumes
\ingroup karma_features
Analytic volumetric-solid feature runtime and system APIs.

Volume spheres are authored as ECS data and rendered through the renderer's
transparent volume path.
*/

/**
\defgroup karma_ui UI
\ingroup karma_features
UI provider adapters that produce runtime UI draw data.

Provider-specific setup belongs under features/ui/provider. The shared engine
contract is karma::app::UiLayer.
*/

/**
\defgroup karma_media Media
Audio-facing APIs and audio system integration.
*/

/**
\defgroup karma_platform Platform
Window events, window abstraction, and network transport APIs.
*/
